{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\textbf{Capability Gap Corollary.}
Let $\Delta = \mathrm{Acc}_V - \mathrm{Acc}_G^{\mathrm{SC}}$ denote the \emph{capability gap} between the NLI verifier's standalone accuracy ($\mathrm{Acc}_V$) and the generator's self-consistency accuracy ($\mathrm{Acc}_G^{\mathrm{SC}}$).
Across our five verifier--generator configurations, combo gain at $w{=}5$ is monotonically increasing in $\Delta$ (Spearman $\rho=1.00$, $p=0.000$; linear $R^2=0.87$).
The fitted linear model predicts a break-even point at $\Delta \approx -42$\,pp; in practice, meaningful gains ($>{+}1$\,pp) emerge only when $\Delta > 0$, i.e., the verifier must \emph{exceed} the generator in standalone capability.
\emph{Caveat:} these trends are estimated from $n{=}5$ configurations and should be interpreted as indicative rather than definitive.
